WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.760
Every neurotrophin binds p75.

2
00:00:06.780 --> 00:00:09.140
So what does p75 do on its own?

3
00:00:10.100 --> 00:00:13.400
In the mid-1990s, two groups found that it can kill.

4
00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:16.860
Barde's group showed this in the developing chick retina.

5
00:00:17.933 --> 00:00:22.773
Chao's group reported that NGF kills cultured rat oligodendrocytes.

6
00:00:23.617 --> 00:00:27.137
These are the myelin-making cells of the brain and spinal cord.

7
00:00:28.017 --> 00:00:29.017
They lack TrkA.

8
00:00:29.767 --> 00:00:34.187
With no Trk receptor to read stay alive, p75 can read die.

9
00:00:35.150 --> 00:00:40.730
The oligodendrocyte experiments were done in culture, where NGF is often added at

10
00:00:40.730 --> 00:00:45.950
nanomolar levels. The retina result involved NGF the tissue makes itself.

11
00:00:47.170 --> 00:00:48.850
Chapter six shows why dose matters.

12
00:00:49.883 --> 00:00:55.723
Yet the precursor has something mature NGF lacks: a pro-domain that sortilin grips,

13
00:00:56.063 --> 00:00:59.143
so a p75 and sortilin pair can hold it tightly.

14
00:01:00.267 --> 00:01:05.547
Like most secreted proteins, NGF is made as a longer chain, proNGF.

15
00:01:06.567 --> 00:01:09.607
The extra stretch at its front is called the pro-domain.

16
00:01:10.667 --> 00:01:14.607
The accepted view had been that the pro-domain is trimmed inside the cell.

17
00:01:15.567 --> 00:01:21.387
In 2001, Ramee Lee, Pouneh Kermani, Kenneth Teng and Barbara Hempstead added to that

18
00:01:21.387 --> 00:01:22.387
picture.

19
00:01:22.767 --> 00:01:25.507
Cells can secrete some proNGF uncut.

20
00:01:26.467 --> 00:01:28.387
Enzymes outside the cell can then cut it.

21
00:01:29.283 --> 00:01:33.323
Plasmin can, a protease made from the blood protein plasminogen.

22
00:01:34.183 --> 00:01:36.503
So can particular matrix metalloproteinases.

23
00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:42.540
To test the precursor alone, mutate its cut sites so proteases cannot trim it.

24
00:01:43.450 --> 00:01:47.830
Take cultured cells with p75 and sortilin, but little TrkA.

25
00:01:48.933 --> 00:01:53.973
Give them mature NGF, or the uncleavable precursor, at the same concentration.

26
00:01:54.953 --> 00:01:58.313
Then block p75. Predict each result.

27
00:02:04.717 --> 00:02:09.137
In this worked example, at a low dose, mature NGF added no death.

28
00:02:09.977 --> 00:02:12.157
The uncleavable precursor killed the cells.

29
00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:16.880
An antibody that blocks p75 prevented that killing.

30
00:02:17.840 --> 00:02:20.180
So did cells from mice lacking p75.

31
00:02:21.283 --> 00:02:25.183
So the precursor kills through p75, without being trimmed.

32
00:02:26.217 --> 00:02:32.377
In Lee's experiments, uncut proNGF also triggered apoptosis of cultured sympathetic

33
00:02:32.377 --> 00:02:33.377
neurons.

34
00:02:33.967 --> 00:02:38.167
It bound p75 tightly, and activated TrkA very little.

35
00:02:39.167 --> 00:02:43.347
Mature NGF kept the same kind of neuron alive, through TrkA.

36
00:02:44.250 --> 00:02:49.470
In 2004, Anders Nykjær, Claus Munck Petersen and colleagues found the missing

37
00:02:49.470 --> 00:02:50.470
partner.

38
00:02:50.933 --> 00:02:54.813
p75 needs sortilin to deliver this death signal.

39
00:02:55.783 --> 00:03:00.303
Sortilin grips the pro-domain, while p75 grips the mature part.

40
00:03:01.233 --> 00:03:04.693
This killing was shown in culture and in rodent injury models.

41
00:03:05.733 --> 00:03:09.273
How much it adds to the normal deaths of development is less clear.

42
00:03:10.233 --> 00:03:15.593
Another group found that uncleavable proNGF kept sympathetic neurons alive, through

43
00:03:15.593 --> 00:03:18.113
TrkA. It did so less well.

44
00:03:19.067 --> 00:03:22.787
The takeaway: p75 can read a neurotrophin as die.

45
00:03:23.587 --> 00:03:26.747
With sortilin, it reads the uncut precursor that way.
